---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Editorial Team | spousalsupportcalc.com';
const description = 'The editorial team behind spousalsupportcalc.com — Harper Quinn and Reese Donovan, spousal support and family law specialists.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Editorial Team</h1>
  <p>spousalsupportcalc.com is written and reviewed by The Click Lab&rsquo;s editorial team before publication. Every formula is verified against state statutes, court rules, and reported family-law practice guidelines. Each content page carries a byline identifying the reviewing editor.</p>

  <h2>Harper Quinn (HQ) &mdash; Editor-in-Chief &amp; Family Law Lead</h2>
  <p>Harper Quinn leads editorial oversight for spousalsupportcalc.com, with primary responsibility for accuracy of state formula research, spousal support duration guidelines, and the judicial discretion factors that adjust formula outcomes. Her background is in family law mediation practice, with particular depth in formula-state calculation mechanics, modification standards across jurisdictions, and the tax treatment changes introduced by the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. She holds a Certified Family Mediator (FCM) credential and reviews all substantive legal and formula content before publication.</p>
  <p>Harper reviews: methodology, calculator formula logic, state guidelines, FAQ, and all pages containing legal analysis of spousal support doctrine.</p>

  <h2>Reese Donovan (RD) &mdash; Contributing Writer, State Guidelines Reference</h2>
  <p>Reese Donovan contributes research and drafting across spousalsupportcalc.com, with a focus on state-specific formula variations, statutory duration rules, and the practical mechanics of spousal support disputes — documentation requirements, the mediation process, forensic accounting in high-asset cases, and modification procedures. Her background as a paralegal in family law practice gives her direct familiarity with how support calculations are developed in real cases, including the discovery processes for establishing income and earning capacity. She drafts initial content for all new pages and updates, which Harper Quinn then reviews and approves.</p>
  <p>Reese contributes: state guidelines research, guide drafts, FAQ updates, and misconceptions content.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every formula and state-specific rule is cited to a primary authority: a state statute, court rule, reported local practice guideline, or published case. We do not present formulas as authoritative without a documented source.</li>
    <li>Calculator formula logic is documented on the <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a> so users can verify the approach and understand its limitations. Any change to formula logic is reviewed by Harper Quinn before deployment.</li>
    <li>State law changes are monitored — Florida&rsquo;s 2023 alimony reform, New Jersey&rsquo;s retirement presumption, and other legislative changes are incorporated into content as they occur. The update date on each page&rsquo;s byline reflects the most recent substantive review.</li>
    <li>No legal advice — this site is informational and educational. Nothing here creates an attorney-client relationship or substitutes for consultation with a family-law attorney in your jurisdiction.</li>
    <li>No paid placements, recommended-attorney lists, or sponsored content. Advertising is served by Google AdSense and does not influence editorial decisions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>Found an error in a formula, a state rule that has changed, or a factual inaccuracy? Email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We review all corrections promptly and update content when errors are confirmed.</p>
</BaseLayout>
